# The upper-half question, asked of every kind that answers it - see `isZeroExtended` and
# `readsLowHalfOnly` in codegen/x64/transform_peephole.cpp.
#
# A widening `cast` from I32 to I64 is `mov eax, eax`, which clears bits 32-63 of its destination -
# and is doing nothing whenever they were clear already. Two rules decide that, and this file is the
# roster of what each of them knows:
#
#   - **backwards**, `isZeroExtended`: the value's definition already wrote a 32-bit register, so
#     every bit above 31 was cleared by the instruction that produced it.
#   - **forwards**, `readsLowHalfOnly`: nothing reads the upper half of the *cast's own result*, so
#     it does not matter what is left there.
#
# Cast.lower beside this file is the pair of rules themselves - the arithmetic, the masks, the loads
# and the two refusals. What is here is every kind that was missing from them: each was added to the
# lower IR, or produced by a transform, after the whitelists were written, and a kind absent from a
# whitelist reads as "no" without saying so anywhere.
#
# What to look for:
#
#   - **every `cast` in a `skip` or `low` function is an empty encoding**, and the *first* one in
#     each `keep` function is a `8bff`-shaped move that stayed - what a `keep` names is the value it
#     is about, and two of them then widen the result of that move, which is clear and skips. That is
#     the whole assertion of the file, in both directions: a move where there should be none is a
#     peephole that stopped working, and none where there should be one is a rule gone unsound.
#   - **`select` is here for a reason of its own.** `cmovcc r32, r/m32` writes its destination
#     whether or not the condition holds - a 32-bit conditional move zero-extends unconditionally,
#     which is exactly why it cannot be used to preserve an upper half. So both arms leave a clear
#     register and the widening after it emits nothing.
#   - **`atomic_load` is an ordinary `mov`.** The ordering an acquire load carries on this
#     architecture is in what may be reordered around it, not in the width it writes, so a four-byte
#     one fills the same register bits a plain load does.
#   - **`x86_storeadd` is the forwards rule, on an instruction no pass wrote.** `selectStoreUpdates`
#     runs above `selectMachineInstructions`, so the store this rule answered for had already been
#     rewritten by the time it was asked - which is the shape of every gap in this file.
#   - **`skipPhi` and `keepPhiOfArgument` are the pair that pins a rule worth nothing.** A phi is as
#     clear as its sources, which is sound and which changes not one byte over the 280 programs of
#     `test/lib` and `test/resolve`; the fixture is the whole of its test.
#   - **`keepSetOfArgument` is the one that was wrong.** A `set` claimed to have written a 32-bit
#     register unconditionally, and a `set` whose source dies is coalesced into nothing at all - so
#     `set %arg; cast to I64` was two empty encodings and a 64-bit read of a register the System V
#     convention describes only the low half of. The rule follows the copy now, which is why
#     `skipThroughSet` beside it still skips: there the source is clear, so the copy is too.
#   - **the other controls each emit their move**, and each for a reason of its own: an argument
#     register and a call's result are the ABI's business above bit 31, an eight-byte atomic load
#     fills the whole register, and a 64-bit `sar` fills the upper half from the sign bit rather than
#     with zeros.

# ## Backwards: definitions that leave a clear register

# The rotations. `ror r/m32, imm8` writes the whole 32-bit destination exactly as `shl` does, and
# `rol` is the same instruction with the count taken the other way round.
skipRotateRight(%x: I32): I64 {
    {
        %r = ror %x, 7: I32
        %w = cast %r: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

skipRotateLeft(%x: I32): I64 {
    {
        %r = rol %x, 7: I32
        %w = cast %r: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# `bswap r32`, which permutes the four bytes of a 32-bit register and writes all of it.
skipByteSwap(%x: I32): I64 {
    {
        %r = bswap %x: I32
        %w = cast %r: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# `crc32 r32, r/m32`. The 64-bit form is the one that would not qualify, and the rule reads the
# result type to tell them apart.
skipCrc32(%acc: I32, %chunk: I32): I64 {
    {
        %r = crc32 %acc, %chunk: I32
        %w = cast %r: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# The conditional move, whose destination is written on both paths.
skipSelect(%c: I32, %a: I32, %b: I32): I64 {
    {
        %cmp = cmp_igt %c, 0: I32
        %s = select %cmp, %a, %b: I32
        %w = cast %s: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# A four-byte atomic load, which is a `mov` with an ordering rule attached to it.
skipAtomicLoad(%p: Ptr): I64 {
    {
        %v = atomic_load %p, 4, acquire: I32
        %w = cast %v: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# The eight-byte one is a 64-bit load and has nothing to be clear about, so the narrowing below it
# stands. This is the control for the case above rather than a case of its own.
keepAtomicLoadWide(%p: Ptr): I64 {
    {
        %v = atomic_load %p, 8, acquire: I64
        %n = cast %v: I32
        %w = cast %n: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# A lane out of a vector, which crosses banks as `pextrd r32` - a general register written whole.
skipVectorLane(%v: i32x4): I64 {
    {
        %l = vlane %v, 2: I32
        %w = cast %l: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# A phi, which is no instruction at all: it is a copy on each incoming edge, and `resolvePhis` gives
# every one of those the class of the *phi's own type* - so an Int32 phi is `mov r32, r32` or a
# 32-bit slot transfer, each of which clears what it does not write. Where the allocator emits no
# copy, the register is the source's - which is why the answer is the sources' and not a claim of the
# phi's own.
#
# Worth nothing measured: over the 280 programs of `test/lib` and `test/resolve` this case changes
# not one byte, and it is here so that the reasoning is pinned rather than because anything needed it.
skipPhi(%c: I32, %a: I32, %b: I32): I64 {
    start {
        je (cmp_igt %c, 0), left, right
    }

    left {
        %x = add %a, %b: I32
        jmp end
    }

    right {
        %y = mul %a, %b: I32
        jmp end
    }

    end {
        %p = phi [left, %x], [right, %y]: I32
        %w = cast %p: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# And the control: one arm carries an argument through unchanged, so the phi is only as clear as
# that - which is not at all.
keepPhiOfArgument(%c: I32, %a: I32, %b: I32): I64 {
    start {
        je (cmp_igt %c, 0), left, right
    }

    left {
        %x = add %a, %b: I32
        jmp end
    }

    right {
        jmp end
    }

    end {
        %p = phi [left, %x], [right, %b]: I32
        %w = cast %p: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# ## Forwards: readers that look no further than bit 31

# The rotations again, from the other side: the narrowing above them has nothing to clear because
# `ror r32` reads 32 bits of its operand and no more.
lowRotate(%x: I64): I32 {
    {
        %n = cast %x: I32
        %r = ror %n, 7: I32
        ret %r
    }
}

lowRotateLeft(%x: I64): I32 {
    {
        %n = cast %x: I32
        %r = rol %n, 7: I32
        ret %r
    }
}

lowByteSwap(%x: I64): I32 {
    {
        %n = cast %x: I32
        %r = bswap %n: I32
        ret %r
    }
}

lowCrc32(%x: I64, %chunk: I32): I32 {
    {
        %n = cast %x: I32
        %r = crc32 %n, %chunk: I32
        ret %r
    }
}

# The read-modify-write. `selectStoreUpdates` has already turned the load, the add and the store into
# one `add [rdi], esi` by the time this is asked, so the rule has to know that instruction and not
# only the `store` it replaced.
lowStoreUpdate(%p: Ptr, %x: I64) {
    {
        %n = cast %x: I32
        %old = load %p, 4: I32
        %new = add %old, %n: I32
        store %p, %new, 4
        ret
    }
}

# And the sign extension `selectSignExtends` builds out of a shift pair, which reads one byte of its
# operand - fewer than the 32 bits the rule needs and therefore inside them.
lowSignExtend(%x: I64): I32 {
    {
        %n = cast %x: I32
        %u = shl %n, 24: I32
        %d = sar %u, 24: I32
        ret %d
    }
}

# ## The controls

# An argument arrives in a register the System V convention says nothing about above bit 31.
keepArgumentWidened(%v: I32): I64 {
    {
        %w = cast %v: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

identity(%x: I32): I32 {
    { ret %x }
}

# Nor does anything promise it of a return register.
keepCallResultWidened(%a: I32): I64 {
    {
        %r = call_complex identity, %a: I32
        %w = cast %r: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# An arithmetic shift fills from the sign bit, so a 64-bit one leaves the upper half full of copies of
# bit 63 and the narrowing below it has real work to do. The *second* cast is skipped for the reason
# the first is not: what it widens is a 32-bit cast's result, which moved at 32 bits and cleared.
#
# Written as a widening rather than as an ordinary 32-bit use, which is what it was first and what
# made it no control at all: a narrowed value read only by a 32-bit `add` is skipped by the forwards
# rule whatever its definition did, so the sar never came into it.
keepArithmeticShift(%x: I64): I64 {
    {
        %s = sar %x, 40L: I64
        %n = cast %s: I32
        %w = cast %n: I64
        ret %w
    }
}

# The one that was wrong. A `set` is a copy, and a copy whose source dies is coalesced into nothing
# at all - so reading it as an instruction that wrote a 32-bit register is reading an instruction
# that is not there. Here the copy is of an argument, whose upper half the System V convention does
# not describe, and the widening after it used to emit nothing: two empty encodings and a 64-bit
# `lea` over a register holding whatever the caller left in it.
#
# The rule follows the copy now, so this is the source's answer: an argument's, which is no.
keepSetOfArgument(%v: I32): I64 {
    {
        %s = set %v: I32
        %w = cast %s: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}

# And the same copy over something that *is* clear, which is what the corpus has and what following
# the source rather than refusing outright keeps: the narrowing above the `set` did the clearing, so
# the widening below it still has nothing to do.
skipThroughSet(%p: Ptr): I64 {
    {
        %v = load %p, 8: I64
        %n = cast %v: I32
        %s = set %n: I32
        %w = cast %s: I64
        %t = add %w, 1L: I64
        ret %t
    }
}
